From: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: sc23xx: report the fallback MAC address as random

When neither the DT, the die UID nor the firmware supply a MAC address the
driver makes one up with eth_random_addr() - and, as its own comment says,
a different one on every boot. It then installs it with eth_hw_addr_set(),
which leaves addr_assign_type at NET_ADDR_PERM, so from userspace it is
indistinguishable from a burned-in address and nothing touches it.

On RG Rotate that is every boot: the SC2355 keeps its address in a
per-unit vendor file on the Android data partition which this driver does
not read. The consequences are what a changing MAC always brings - a new
DHCP lease each time the board comes up, and a stale ARP entry on every
host that talked to the previous boot, which shows up as "No route to
host" until the entry ages out.

Flag the fallback as NET_ADDR_RANDOM. That is what the flag is for:
systemd-udevd's default MACAddressPolicy=persistent (99-default.link) then
replaces it before the interface is brought up with an address derived
from the machine-id and the interface name, stable for the life of the
install. The driver already forwards whatever the netdev holds to the
firmware at open time, so nothing else is needed. Addresses that did come
from the platform or the firmware are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/main.c b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/main.c
index c54bd0f88a1b..7b10dd680801 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/main.c
@@ -657,6 +657,7 @@ int sc23xx_register_device(struct sc23xx_dev *sdev)
 	 */
 	if (is_zero_ether_addr(sdev->mac_addr)) {
 		eth_random_addr(sdev->mac_addr);
+		sdev->mac_addr_random = true;
 		wiphy_info(sdev->wiphy, "using random MAC address %pM\n",
 			   sdev->mac_addr);
 	}
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/sc23xx.h b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/sc23xx.h
index 6198a942994d..bbd52960f7e8 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/sc23xx.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/sc23xx.h
@@ -114,6 +114,7 @@ struct sc23xx_dev {
 	u8 credit_capa;
 
 	u8 mac_addr[ETH_ALEN];
+	bool mac_addr_random;
 	struct ieee80211_supported_band band_2ghz;
 	struct ieee80211_supported_band band_5ghz;
 	u8 max_mc_mac_addrs;
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/vif.c b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/vif.c
index 98a2064cbc51..72b4344b4e37 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/vif.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/unisoc/vif.c
@@ -252,6 +252,16 @@ struct sc23xx_vif *sc23xx_vif_new(struct sc23xx_dev *sdev, const char *name,
 	if (test_bit(SC23XX_FLAG_USE_ALT_MAC, &vif->flags))
 		mac_addr[5] ^= 0x70;
 	eth_hw_addr_set(ndev, mac_addr);
+	/*
+	 * A made-up address has to be reported as such, or userspace takes it
+	 * for a burned-in one and leaves it alone. With NET_ADDR_RANDOM set,
+	 * systemd's default MACAddressPolicy=persistent replaces it before the
+	 * interface comes up with one derived from the machine-id, so the
+	 * address - and with it the DHCP lease and every peer's ARP entry -
+	 * stops changing on each boot.
+	 */
+	if (sdev->mac_addr_random)
+		ndev->addr_assign_type = NET_ADDR_RANDOM;
 
 	ret = cfg80211_register_netdevice(ndev);
 	if (ret)
